# Line endings are a ship contract here, not a formatting preference.
#
# harness-kit's mechanism is parsed line-by-line (kit-manifest,
# .harness-manifest, .harness/gates.conf) and executed by bash. Git for Windows
# defaults to core.autocrlf=true, so an ordinary Windows clone of a repo with no
# .gitattributes rewrites all of it to CRLF. That turns the manifest's blank
# separators into lone-CR records — which awk and `read` both see as a non-blank
# field — and appends a CR to every trailing field, so `bootstrap install` dies
# with ~100 `unknown layer '<CR>'` errors before it copies anything. Pinning the
# rule here means the repository enforces it, instead of every contributor
# having to hold their git config right.

# Everything text checks out LF on every platform. `text=auto` keeps git's
# binary detection, so this stays correct if a binary asset is ever added.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Named explicitly so that narrowing the blanket rule above can never silently
# un-protect the files that actually break. These patterns match by basename at
# any depth, which covers both sides of the mechanism: the shipped templates
# under plugins/ (the product) and this repo's own installed copy (dogfood).
*.sh              eol=lf
kit-manifest      eol=lf
.harness-manifest eol=lf
gates.conf        eol=lf

# Windows-native launchers would genuinely want CRLF. None exist today — the
# kit's hooks assume a POSIX shell — but the rule belongs here so that adding
# one later does not require rediscovering this file.
*.cmd eol=crlf
*.bat eol=crlf
*.ps1 eol=crlf
